### Plan Execution Rules

1. **Subagent-driven execution by default** - Execute plans by spawning a fresh subagent per task (see `.codex/skills/superpowers/subagent-driven-development/SKILL.md`). Use inline execution (`executing-plans`) only when the user explicitly requests it.
2. **Plan files are not committed** - `docs/superpowers/plans/*.md` must NOT be committed.
3. **Plan file lifecycle** - Kept locally during implementation, deleted after PR merge.

**Review consolidation** (quality policy §5.5 - review loops exist at exactly two points: document creation and the merge gate):

4. **No in-development reviews** - During plan execution (subagent or inline), do NOT spawn spec-conformance or quality reviewers for individual tasks. This overrides **both** review stages in the subagent-driven-development skill: the per-task two-stage review loop **and the final whole-branch code review at plan end** (including its fix-dispatch / scoped re-review follow-up) - the latter covers the same full diff that the merge-gate quality-check Step 4 already reviews, so running it would be a duplicate. The implementer's self-review (run the tests, verify the diff against the task text) is the only per-task check; the orchestrating session spot-checks the diff and moves on. Quality assurance happens once, at the merge-gate quality-check.
5. **Document reviews stay** - Design and plan reviews (`gpt-5.6-sol` / `high`) stay: normally one round; extra rounds only for high findings or large documents, with the cap in quality-policy §5.5. **After the plan review, do not ask the user for confirmation - start implementation.** Designs that change which exceptions are caught vs. propagated follow the caller-trace item in quality-policy §5.5.
6. **Subagent budget in the plan** - Every implementation plan states an expected subagent-dispatch count (target and cap); escalate to the user when the run is projected to exceed it.
7. **The merge gate is not relaxed** - The final quality-check gate (Step 4) keeps its own rule that every High/Medium finding is addressed. If the gate produces many findings, do not reinstate per-task reviews; route the causes instead (mechanically detectable issues to the lint layer, design-level issues into the spec/plan review checklists).
8. **Platform constraints are confirmed first** - Designs or verifications that rely on a platform constraint (rate limits, execution time caps, size limits) MUST confirm the constraint against official documentation before work starts - never assume a limit from memory.
9. **Interrupted subagents are resumed, not re-dispatched** - Separate committed from uncommitted work first (`git log --oneline` / `git status --short`), restate file ownership to the resumed agent (the prohibition on `git checkout` / `restore` / `stash` / `clean` on files it doesn't own), and record the interruption in `_notes` (see the `quality-check` skill's subagent-interruption resume procedure).

### Subagent Model Selection

**When spawning subagents, always specify `model` and `model_reasoning_effort` explicitly based on the nature of the task. Do not rely on inheriting the parent session settings.**

**This table takes precedence over any model-selection guidance inside individual skills** (e.g. the "Model Selection" section of `.codex/skills/superpowers/subagent-driven-development/SKILL.md`, which assumes a cheapest-model-per-role strategy). When they conflict, follow this table.

| Task Type | Model | Reasoning Effort | Examples |
|-----------|-------|------------------|----------|
| Initial planning / design framing | `gpt-5.6-sol` | `high` | First-pass requirements decomposition, architecture option framing, implementation strategy design |
| Codebase exploration / context gathering | `gpt-5.4-mini` | `medium` | Understanding project structure during planning, read-heavy scans, finding related implementations |
| Simple mechanical search | `gpt-5.4-mini` | `low` | File/symbol lookup, listing usages, collecting file paths |
| Implementation / concrete work | `gpt-5.6-terra` (alternative: `gpt-5.6-sol` with lowered effort) | `medium` | Task-by-task implementation subagents, test writing, refactoring, migrations |
| Design / plan review (spec-document and plan-document reviewers) | `gpt-5.6-sol` | `high` | Reviewing the design doc or implementation plan itself |
| Pure documentation review (README / `documents/` wording, no design judgment) | `gpt-5.4-mini` | `medium` | Reviewing README or `documents/` prose with no design decision involved |
| Quality / architecture review, quality-check personas | `gpt-5.6-sol` | `high` | 6-persona quality-check review, code review, architecture critique, security analysis |

Rules:

1. **Planning/design subagents** -> `gpt-5.6-sol` with `high` effort. This is the Codex-side counterpart of Fable in Claude Code.
2. **Exploration subagents** (read-only research during planning) -> `gpt-5.4-mini` with `medium` effort. Speed matters more than depth; results are distilled back to the main session.
3. **Implementation subagents** (task executors in subagent-driven execution) -> `gpt-5.6-terra`, or `gpt-5.6-sol` with `model_reasoning_effort` lowered to `medium`/`low` (a design and a plan exist; implementation is mechanical). Do not spend high-effort frontier models on routine implementation.
4. **Review subagents are split by review type** (quality policy §5.5, model tiering): design/plan reviews run on `gpt-5.6-sol` / `high` (the same tier as the author); pure documentation reviews (no design judgment) run on `gpt-5.4-mini` / `medium`. **The quality-check personas** run on `gpt-5.6-sol` with `high` effort, because that review directly gates push/merge. Per-task reviewers are not spawned at all (Plan Execution Rule 4). Never downgrade the quality-check personas to a mini model.
5. If reusable, define these as project agents under `.codex/agents/` (e.g. `implementer.toml` with `model = "gpt-5.6-terra"`, `reviewer.toml` with `model = "gpt-5.6-sol"` / `model_reasoning_effort = "high"` / `sandbox_mode = "read-only"`). Otherwise, state the model explicitly in the spawn prompt.
6. When in doubt between two tiers, choose the more capable model.

